=== the published Java encode regression, reconstructed ===

The prior Java slice published encode at 1.08 to 1.84 of protobuf-java, "a regression on
every payload", and this slice's whole question was whether that survives ABI v1. Measured
against a message serialised ONCE -- which is what an application does -- it does not:
`ffi-take` is 0.58 to 0.96 on every real element-bearing payload.

What follows is not a refutation of that report. It is one harness choice, undocumented in
any of the three published reports, that reproduces its band from this slice's own numbers.
protobuf-java memoizes `getSerializedSize()` on the message instance, so a loop over one
message pays the size pass once; the `pbj-loop` column is exactly that arm.

| payload | ffi-take vs a message serialised once | pbj-loop | ffi-take vs a LOOP |
|---|---|---|---|
| P1.1 | 0.802 | 0.517 | 1.551 |
| P1.2 | 0.738 | 0.529 | 1.395 |
| P2.1 | 0.957 | 0.516 | 1.855 |
| P2.2 | 0.875 | 0.538 | 1.626 |
| P2.3 | 0.643 | 0.484 | 1.329 |
| P2.4 | 0.708 | 0.525 | 1.349 |
| P2.5 | 0.930 | 0.514 | 1.809 |
| P3.1 | 0.576 | 0.457 | 1.260 |
| P4.1 | 0.760 | 0.433 | 1.755 |

**1.26 to 1.85, against a published 1.08 to 1.84.**

Read it as what it is: a mechanism that would produce the published shape, measured here,
and not a claim about what the prior harness did. Its sources do not survive (README W6:
"there is nothing to import"), so nothing can settle it. What it does settle is that this
slice must say which baseline every figure is against, because the two differ by more than
the effect either report is about.
